
               Accepting AI output as contribution to libburnia

Contributions generated by artificial intelligence (AI) are welcome under
conditions. In any case they need to be marked as such.

A main problem with contributions generated by AI is the question of copyright.
In the project parts which are under GPL, LGPL, or BSD license we normally
avoid this question by not importing copyright from contributions.

Every contribution to future releases of libisofs, libburn, or libisoburn has
to go through the hands of a current libburnia copyright holder (see README)
who will then claim the possible copyright for the resulting change in the
repositories of these libraries. Either the possible copyright of a
contribution gets transfered by a statement like "Signed-off-by:" or the
libburnia copyright holder will invest enough own work in the repository
modification to justify this claim.

Since AI most probably cannot claim or transfer copyright, all AI contributions
are considered as reports and proposals, not as changes which get applied
after some review. Therefore they have to be marked as AI output and must
not exceed a human's capability to understand them within reasonable time.

Other repositories of the project might be open to contributions with other
copyright or with no attributed copyright. This includes test suites which
were generated by AI.
If legitmate copyright holders of content of such repositories demand it, the
respective content will be removed.


              How to mark contributions which contain AI output

Tell in the explanation of your contribution which AIs were involved and,
if you are one or more human beings, how much you were involved in the
development.

Consider to choose one of the following characterizations:

0: No human was involded. You are an AI yourself.
   (No "Signed-off-by:" acceptable.)

1: A single AI prompt by you led to the contribution.
   (No "Signed-off-by:" acceptable.)

2: A multi-step dialog between you and AI led to the contribution.
   (No "Signed-off-by:" acceptable.)

3: At some point AI involvement ended and substantial further development
   was done by you.
   ("Signed-off-by:" may be acceptable, but needs justification why the
    contribution surely does not contain AI emitted text with foreign
    copyright.)

4: The contribution is predominantly by you. AI was only used to get ideas and
   proposals how to improve it.
   (Please transfer possible copyright claims by "Signed-off-by:")

5: No AI was involved specifically. There is still the possibility that
   AI was involved in the search for the knowledge which was needed to
   develop the contribution.
   (This is considered a classical contribution by humans.
    Please transfer possible copyright claims by "Signed-off-by:")

